today on campaign check labour says it  will begin a wave of nationalizations in  the first 100 days of its term in office  the party's promised to bring rail mail  water energy and broadband all into  public ownership in its manifesto it  says the move will be fiscally mutual in  other words it won't cost the public  anything but it's that true let's take a  look at the figures first buying these  businesses will cost at least 50 billion  pounds that's the price of compensating  the current owners but these companies  also have existing debts of around 150  billion pounds but labour argues that's  all outweighed by the assets being added  to the government's balance sheet in  other words the value of the companies  it's acquiring estimated to also be  around 200 billion pounds so the numbers  add up right well in theory yes but  let's take a closer look first at that  50 billion figure the Shadow Chancellor  John McDonald has said labour wouldn't  sign a blank cheque for these businesses  the problem is that investors are  unlikely to be happy with just that 50  billion because it's what's known as The  Book value not the market value of the  businesses to take the water company  seventh rent as an example its Book  value in other words the physical value  of the assets like buildings and  infrastructure is around one point to  three billion but its market value which  takes into account things like future  profit is nearly five times that much at  more than five billion if Labour does  try and pay less than market value that  could pave way for legal action and it  could cost them dearly for example both  SSC and National Grid have set up  holding companies in jurisdictions like  Switzerland and Luxembourg where  treaties mean they'll have a better  chance in any potential future legal  action the real question though is  whether these businesses will be run  better in the public sector than they  were in the private sector if they make  big profits they'll be shared amongst  all of us but if they make losses the  taxpayer could be on the hook take the  rail industry as an example labour have  already pledged to cut rail fares by 33  percent at a  of about 1.5 billion pounds a cost that  wasn't in the labor manifesto and while  they say the money will be taken from a  road repair fund that will still mean  either 1.5 billion less gets spent on  roads or it needs to be raised in extra  taxes or borrowing so while it might be  possible to nationalize at no cost it's  by no means guaranteed and owning these  public utilities will expose the  taxpayer to potential risks as well as  potential game
 

